WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:07.870
What I have attempted to
draw here is a unit circle.

00:07.870 --> 00:10.460
And the fact I'm
calling it a unit circle

00:10.460 --> 00:13.190
means it has a radius of 1.

00:13.190 --> 00:15.290
So this length from
the center-- and I

00:15.290 --> 00:19.230
centered it at the origin--
this length, from the center

00:19.230 --> 00:23.560
to any point on the
circle, is of length 1.

00:23.560 --> 00:26.890
So what would this coordinate
be right over there, right

00:26.890 --> 00:30.150
where it intersects
along the x-axis?

00:30.150 --> 00:33.910
Well, x would be
1, y would be 0.

00:33.910 --> 00:35.770
What would this
coordinate be up here?

00:35.770 --> 00:38.420
Well, we've gone 1
above the origin,

00:38.420 --> 00:40.450
but we haven't moved to
the left or the right.

00:40.450 --> 00:41.860
So our x value is 0.

00:41.860 --> 00:43.420
Our y value is 1.

00:43.420 --> 00:44.930
What about back here?

00:44.930 --> 00:47.970
Well, here our x value is -1.

00:47.970 --> 00:49.200
We've moved 1 to the left.

00:49.200 --> 00:52.140
And we haven't moved up or
down, so our y value is 0.

00:52.140 --> 00:53.520
And what about down here?

00:53.520 --> 00:58.670
Well, we've gone a unit
down, or 1 below the origin.

00:58.670 --> 01:00.800
But we haven't moved
in the xy direction.

01:00.800 --> 01:04.810
So our x is 0, and
our y is negative 1.

01:04.810 --> 01:08.230
Now, with that out of the way,
I'm going to draw an angle.

01:08.230 --> 01:10.510
And the way I'm going
to draw this angle--

01:10.510 --> 01:13.160
I'm going to define a
convention for positive angles.

01:13.160 --> 01:16.200
I'm going to say a
positive angle-- well,

01:16.200 --> 01:18.110
the initial side
of the angle we're

01:18.110 --> 01:20.500
always going to do along
the positive x-axis.

01:20.500 --> 01:24.320
So you can kind of view
it as the starting side,

01:24.320 --> 01:26.805
the initial side of an angle.

01:26.805 --> 01:31.090
And then to draw a positive
angle, the terminal side,

01:31.090 --> 01:35.820
we're going to move in a
counterclockwise direction.

01:35.820 --> 01:38.470
So positive angle means
we're going counterclockwise.

01:41.739 --> 01:43.780
And this is just the
convention I'm going to use,

01:43.780 --> 01:46.160
and it's also the convention
that is typically used.

01:46.160 --> 01:47.860
And so you can imagine
a negative angle

01:47.860 --> 01:51.950
would move in a
clockwise direction.

01:51.950 --> 01:54.602
So let me draw a positive angle.

01:54.602 --> 01:59.510
So a positive angle might
look something like this.

01:59.510 --> 02:00.770
This is the initial side.

02:00.770 --> 02:05.510
And then from that, I go in
a counterclockwise direction

02:05.510 --> 02:07.530
until I measure out the angle.

02:07.530 --> 02:09.250
And then this is
the terminal side.

02:09.250 --> 02:11.720
So this is a
positive angle theta.

02:11.720 --> 02:14.830
And what I want to do is
think about this point

02:14.830 --> 02:16.920
of intersection
between the terminal

02:16.920 --> 02:19.620
side of this angle
and my unit circle.

02:19.620 --> 02:23.200
And let's just say it has
the coordinates a comma b.

02:23.200 --> 02:25.370
The x value where
it intersects is a.

02:25.370 --> 02:28.550
The y value where
it intersects is b.

02:28.550 --> 02:31.330
And the whole point
of what I'm doing here

02:31.330 --> 02:33.720
is I'm going to see how
this unit circle might

02:33.720 --> 02:37.720
be able to help us extend our
traditional definitions of trig

02:37.720 --> 02:38.220
functions.

02:38.220 --> 02:39.970
And so what I want
to do is I want

02:39.970 --> 02:43.900
to make this theta part
of a right triangle.

02:43.900 --> 02:45.760
So to make it part
of a right triangle,

02:45.760 --> 02:49.660
let me drop an altitude
right over here.

02:49.660 --> 02:54.550
And let me make it clear that
this is a 90-degree angle.

02:54.550 --> 02:58.030
So this theta is part
of this right triangle.

02:58.030 --> 03:00.050
So let's see what
we can figure out

03:00.050 --> 03:03.350
about the sides of
this right triangle.

03:03.350 --> 03:05.630
So the first question
I have to ask

03:05.630 --> 03:11.740
you is, what is the
length of the hypotenuse

03:11.740 --> 03:15.130
of this right triangle that
I have just constructed?

03:15.130 --> 03:19.500
Well, this hypotenuse is just
a radius of a unit circle.

03:19.500 --> 03:21.250
The unit circle
has a radius of 1.

03:21.250 --> 03:24.770
So the hypotenuse has length 1.

03:24.770 --> 03:29.480
Now, what is the length of
this blue side right over here?

03:29.480 --> 03:33.530
You could view this as the
opposite side to the angle.

03:33.530 --> 03:38.570
Well, this height is
the exact same thing

03:38.570 --> 03:42.880
as the y-coordinate of
this point of intersection.

03:42.880 --> 03:46.380
So this height right over here
is going to be equal to b.

03:46.380 --> 03:48.370
The y-coordinate
right over here is b.

03:48.370 --> 03:50.690
This height is equal to b.

03:50.690 --> 03:54.060
Now, exact same logic--
what is the length

03:54.060 --> 03:55.570
of this base going to be?

03:55.570 --> 03:59.540
The base just of
the right triangle?

03:59.540 --> 04:02.780
Well, this is going
to be the x-coordinate

04:02.780 --> 04:04.082
of this point of intersection.

04:04.082 --> 04:05.540
If you were to drop
this down, this

04:05.540 --> 04:07.500
is the point x is equal to a.

04:07.500 --> 04:14.780
Or this whole length between the
origin and that is of length a.

04:14.780 --> 04:17.460
Now that we have
set that up, what

04:17.460 --> 04:24.580
is the cosine-- let me
use the same green-- what

04:24.580 --> 04:29.510
is the cosine of my angle going
to be in terms of a's and b's

04:29.510 --> 04:32.460
and any other numbers
that might show up?

04:32.460 --> 04:34.630
Well, to think
about that, we just

04:34.630 --> 04:36.310
need our soh cah toa definition.

04:36.310 --> 04:37.870
That's the only one we have now.

04:37.870 --> 04:41.380
We are actually in the process
of extending it-- soh cah toa

04:41.380 --> 04:44.370
definition of trig functions.

04:44.370 --> 04:46.770
And the cah part is what
helps us with cosine.

04:46.770 --> 04:48.880
It tells us that the
cosine of an angle

04:48.880 --> 04:51.220
is equal to the length
of the adjacent side

04:51.220 --> 04:52.750
over the hypotenuse.

04:52.750 --> 04:54.310
So what's this going to be?

04:54.310 --> 04:56.410
The length of the
adjacent side--

04:56.410 --> 04:59.280
for this angle, the
adjacent side has length a.

04:59.280 --> 05:02.050
So it's going to be
equal to a over-- what's

05:02.050 --> 05:03.580
the length of the hypotenuse?

05:03.580 --> 05:05.090
Well, that's just 1.

05:05.090 --> 05:09.740
So the cosine of theta
is just equal to a.

05:09.740 --> 05:11.320
Let me write this down again.

05:11.320 --> 05:15.450
So the cosine of theta
is just equal to a.

05:15.450 --> 05:18.070
It's equal to the x-coordinate
of where this terminal

05:18.070 --> 05:21.340
side of the angle
intersected the unit circle.

05:21.340 --> 05:25.320
Now let's think about
the sine of theta.

05:25.320 --> 05:28.600
And I'm going to do it in-- let
me see-- I'll do it in orange.

05:28.600 --> 05:34.286
So what's the sine
of theta going to be?

05:34.286 --> 05:37.330
Well, we just have to look at
the soh part of our soh cah toa

05:37.330 --> 05:38.300
definition.

05:38.300 --> 05:41.470
It tells us that sine is
opposite over hypotenuse.

05:41.470 --> 05:44.950
Well, the opposite
side here has length b.

05:44.950 --> 05:48.460
And the hypotenuse has length 1.

05:48.460 --> 05:54.890
So our sine of
theta is equal to b.

05:54.890 --> 05:57.540
So an interesting
thing-- this coordinate,

05:57.540 --> 06:00.050
this point where our
terminal side of our angle

06:00.050 --> 06:01.940
intersected the
unit circle, that

06:01.940 --> 06:05.344
point a, b-- we could
also view this as a

06:05.344 --> 06:07.363
is the same thing
as cosine of theta.

06:10.250 --> 06:16.300
And b is the same
thing as sine of theta.

06:16.300 --> 06:17.810
Well, that's interesting.

06:17.810 --> 06:20.650
We just used our soh
cah toa definition.

06:20.650 --> 06:24.190
Now, can we in some way use
this to extend soh cah toa?

06:24.190 --> 06:26.020
Because soh cah
toa has a problem.

06:26.020 --> 06:30.090
It works out fine if our angle
is greater than 0 degrees,

06:30.090 --> 06:31.840
if we're dealing with
degrees, and if it's

06:31.840 --> 06:33.370
less than 90 degrees.

06:33.370 --> 06:36.620
We can always make it
part of a right triangle.

06:36.620 --> 06:39.190
But soh cah toa
starts to break down

06:39.190 --> 06:43.130
as our angle is either 0 or
maybe even becomes negative,

06:43.130 --> 06:46.330
or as our angle is
90 degrees or more.

06:46.330 --> 06:48.890
You can't have a right triangle
with two 90-degree angles

06:48.890 --> 06:49.390
in it.

06:49.390 --> 06:50.750
It starts to break down.

06:50.750 --> 06:52.540
Let me make this clear.

06:52.540 --> 06:55.540
So sure, this is
a right triangle,

06:55.540 --> 06:57.040
so the angle is pretty large.

06:57.040 --> 06:59.322
I can make the angle even
larger and still have

06:59.322 --> 07:00.030
a right triangle.

07:00.030 --> 07:03.741
Even larger-- but I can never
get quite to 90 degrees.

07:03.741 --> 07:05.240
At 90 degrees, it's
not clear that I

07:05.240 --> 07:06.531
have a right triangle any more.

07:06.531 --> 07:07.979
It all seems to break down.

07:07.979 --> 07:09.520
And especially the
case, what happens

07:09.520 --> 07:12.380
when I go beyond 90 degrees.

07:12.380 --> 07:16.230
So let's see if we can
use what we said up here.

07:16.230 --> 07:19.230
Let's set up a new definition
of our trig functions

07:19.230 --> 07:23.290
which is really an
extension of soh cah toa

07:23.290 --> 07:25.050
and is consistent
with soh cah toa.

07:25.050 --> 07:28.530
Instead of defining cosine as
if I have a right triangle,

07:28.530 --> 07:30.780
and saying, OK, it's the
adjacent over the hypotenuse.

07:30.780 --> 07:32.660
Sine is the opposite
over the hypotenuse.

07:32.660 --> 07:34.560
Tangent is opposite
over adjacent.

07:34.560 --> 07:37.520
Why don't I just
say, for any angle,

07:37.520 --> 07:41.280
I can draw it in the unit circle
using this convention that I

07:41.280 --> 07:42.270
just set up?

07:42.270 --> 07:48.160
And let's just say that
the cosine of our angle

07:48.160 --> 07:56.270
is equal to the x-coordinate
where we intersect,

07:56.270 --> 07:59.402
where the terminal
side of our angle

07:59.402 --> 08:00.950
intersects the unit circle.

08:17.180 --> 08:20.990
And why don't we
define sine of theta

08:20.990 --> 08:25.350
to be equal to the
y-coordinate where the terminal

08:25.350 --> 08:28.060
side of the angle
intersects the unit circle?

08:28.060 --> 08:30.720
So essentially, for
any angle, this point

08:30.720 --> 08:34.237
is going to define cosine
of theta and sine of theta.

08:34.237 --> 08:36.070
And so what would be a
reasonable definition

08:36.070 --> 08:37.240
for tangent of theta?

08:37.240 --> 08:40.350
Well, tangent of theta--
even with soh cah toa--

08:40.350 --> 08:42.620
could be defined
as sine of theta

08:42.620 --> 08:45.750
over cosine of theta,
which in this case

08:45.750 --> 08:48.990
is just going to be the
y-coordinate where we intersect

08:48.990 --> 08:53.650
the unit circle over
the x-coordinate.

08:53.650 --> 08:56.310
In the next few videos,
I'll show some examples

08:56.310 --> 08:59.210
where we use the unit
circle definition to start

08:59.210 --> 09:03.194
evaluating some trig ratios.
